"""zxv-get - download the ZXV golden discs for your machine from the bucket.

    tools/zxv-get                      core disc for this machine + every swarm disc
    tools/zxv-get --arch arm64         another architecture's core disc
    tools/zxv-get --list               what the bucket holds, download nothing
    tools/zxv-get --core-only          only the core disc (it boots alone)

Disc 1, the core disc, is the only one tied to an architecture (x86_64,
arm64, riscv64); the swarm discs 2..N are the same for all and stack on top
of it. Each is at most 1.8 GB.

Where from. The bucket is the CID in build_system/zxv_upstream.cid (the one
the core discs carry), or --cid. If that file names an IPNS key (or --ipns
is given) and a local IPFS node is running, the newest bucket is resolved
through it; a public gateway cannot prove an IPNS answer here, so without a
node the CID is used and that is said.

How. With a local node (`ipfs` on PATH and its daemon or repo usable, or
--ipfs), files come through `ipfs cat`, which checks every block. Otherwise
from gateways (--gateway, repeatable; default the upstream file's gateway
lines, then https://trustless-gateway.link), one raw block at a time: each
block is hashed and must match its CID before it is used, so no gateway can
change a byte. The gateway that served last is asked first, then the others
in turn; one that sends a block that does not match is not asked again. If
none can, attach the discs by hand. Either way every disc is then checked
against manifest.json (size and SHA-256) and against the CID the bucket's
directory gives it. A disc already present with the right SHA-256 is kept,
not fetched again.

Elastic. Fetching can be slow, so it waits rather than gives up: when no
gateway serves a block it says it is waiting for peers and tries again
after 1 s, then 2, 4 ... up to a minute between tries, with no overall
deadline (--patience SECONDS sets one). A slow gateway's timeout doubles
(30 s up to 4 min) and eases back once it answers. Ctrl-C, a dropped link
or a gateway that lies leaves the verified part as <disc>.part; the next run
hashes it again (it is never trusted unread) and fetches only the rest. A
disc whose finished SHA-256 does not match is deleted, never left behind;
one resumed from a .part is first fetched once more from the start.

Needs only Python 3.8+. Writes into --out (default ./zxv-discs).
"""

